WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.600
There might be times when you want to combine&nbsp;
two datasets into one but with no logical way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.520
or reason to join the two tables. You may want to&nbsp;
stack a list of records, one on top of the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.920
To do this, we use a UNION keyword in between&nbsp;
two SELECT statements. Notice that column names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.080
are not quite the same on each SELECT statement.&nbsp;
They don't need to be, but you will want to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.560
sure you line the data up so that one set of&nbsp;
records stacks neatly on top of the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.920
I have this set to ORDER BY RecruitID, which&nbsp;
is a sort, and I will execute this query.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.720
Notice that it would seem we have duplicates, but&nbsp;
we do not. Remember that this shows a combined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.720
set of two recordsets, so there are bound to be&nbsp;
some duplicates among individual columns. Whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.080
UNION stacks two recordsets into one output,&nbsp;
the INTERSECT keyword shows records that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:02.080
alike in both tables. I will change the keyword&nbsp;
from UNION to INTERSECT and execute the query.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.240
It turns out that one person is in both&nbsp;
tables. To be considered an intersect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.440
each column in the query must have identical&nbsp;
values in both tables. The real-life application&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.400
here is that we would need to figure&nbsp;
out if this person is a recruit or not.

